#include "../include/head.hh"

<body>
		#include "../include/header.hh"
		#include "../include/elec_header.hh"

		MARK_ACTIVE("ee88")

	<link rel="stylesheet" href="/e88.css">
		<link rel="stylesheet" href="/index.css">

		<article id="eagle88_stuff">
			<h1>Eagle-88 computer</h1>	
			<img src="/projects/e881.jpg" alt="eagle88 computer running demo slideshow program" style="max-width: 100%">
			<h2>History</h2>
			<div class="art_content">
				<p>
				My idea of building an 8088 based computer can be traced back to 2020. While I did manage to build a small portion of it at first, it did not have any input or output capabilities and I was unable to test it. 
				</p>
				<p>
				Fast forward to 2023: thanks to the EPQ program and my school at the time's electronics lab, I was able add video output to the computer and actually check whether my design actually worked. And it did! It worked almost flawlessly and the software I had coded in the emulator ran on real hardware most of the time, which included a port of WOZMON, and a rudimentary version of Tetris. Later on I would also write a version of BASIC for the computer. 
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="art_content">
				

			<h3>Reasons for build</h3>
			<ul>
				<li>One of my life goals</li>
				<li>Highly educational and gives me bragging rights</li>
				<li>I'm a masochist and I hate myself</li>
					
			</ul>

			<h3>Specifications</h3>
			<ul>
				<li>384 KiB static ram</li>
				<li>80c88 or compatible processor @6.29375 Mhz</li>
				<li>320x480 monochrome vga framebuffer "graphics card"</li>
				<li>Optional "Hermes" sound card with SAA1099 & AY8910 sound chips</li>	
				<li>Bitbang SD-CARD support</li>
				<li>Rudimentary memory protection for possible unix support????</li>
			</ul>
	</div>

	</div>


</body>
